models/probe_models/base.py:89-111 (`Probe.forward`) moves only input_ids, attention_mask, event_end and position_ids to `next(self.backbone.parameters()).device` and returns `Outputs.logits` on that device. train/utils/trainer.py:175 sets `device = "cuda" if torch.cuda.is_available() else "cpu"` and passes it to both `base.load` calls, and base.load ends with `return instance.to(device)`, so on any launched train run the logits are CUDA tensors. The three methods build their other tensors with bare `torch.tensor(...)` / `torch.zeros(...)`, i.e. on CPU, and never move them:
- ctool.py:210 `y = torch.tensor([probe.labels.index(t) ...])` then :211 `F.cross_entropy(out.logits.float(), y, ...)` and :212 `(ce * batch["weight"]).sum()`;
- cgen.py:224 `F.cross_entropy(out.logits.float(), batch["target_ids"], ...)`, :227-228 `torch.zeros(n_rows).index_add(0, row_of, tok_ce)`;
- cparam.py:246, :249-250 the same two lines.
ctool.reference_loss (:230-233) has the identical split. `grep -n "\.to(" train/methods/*.py train/utils/trainer.py` returns nothing, and the only callers of `method.loss` are trainer.py:271 (the step loop) and trainer.py:369 (the alignment gate), both of which hand the batch through untouched, so nothing moves it. With `train.align_check` true (schema.py:112 default) the crash lands inside `_run_alignment_gate` before the first optimizer step; with it false it lands on the first training block. cgen/cparam `_weighted_val_ce` (cgen.py:289, cparam.py:313) calls `loss` too, so validation crashes as well. The T13 report's own round-2 evidence records `devices_seen ['cuda', 'cuda']` on this host, so `device="cuda"` is what a real launch produces. Every CPU acceptance command (A3.3, A3.4, A3.6, tests/test_packed_loss.py) stubs or builds a CPU-only probe, which is why none of them sees this; GPU acceptance item 1 is still BLOCKED, so it has never been exercised.

